     "text": [
      "```python\n",
      "def hello_world():\n",
      "  \"\"\"Prints 'Hello, World!' to the console.\"\"\"\n",
      "  print(\"Hello, World!\")\n",
      "\n",
      "# Example usage:\n",
      "hello_world()\n",
      "```\n",
      "\n",
      "**Explanation:**\n",
      "\n",
      "* **`def hello_world():`**:  This defines a function named `hello_world`.  The parentheses `()` indicate that it takes no arguments.\n",
      "* **`\"\"\"Prints 'Hello, World!' to the console.\"\"\"`**: This is a docstring. It's a multi-line string used to document what the function does.\n",
      "* **`print(\"Hello, World!\")`**: This is the core of the function.  The `print()` function displays the text \"Hello, World!\" to the console (your screen).\n",
      "* **`hello_world()`**: This line *calls* the function, executing its code.\n",
      "\n",
      "**How to Run the Code:**\n",
      "\n",
      "1.  **Save the code:** Save the code above as a file named `hello.py`.\n",
      "2.  **Run from the command line:** Open a terminal or command prompt, navigate to the directory where you saved the file, and run the command: `python hello.py`\n",
      "\n",
      "This will print \"Hello, World!\" to your console.\n"
     ]
    }
